title: Audit what still has to be done by hand
effort: M
area: cli
created: 2026-09-17T06:54:30Z
updated: 2026-09-17T07:03:09Z

Two properties, and they are not the same one:

- Everything must be possible without pidly installed. A checkout handed to
  someone else is navigable with find, sed, mv and an editor. This is
  principle 1 and it is already true.
- Nothing should require it. With the package installed, a human or an agent
  should never have to reach past the CLI to get ordinary work done. This is
  not true today and nobody has ever listed the gaps.

The audit is the inverse of the reference: every hand idiom documented in
.pidly/README.md is a claim about what the format can do, so each one should
have a command beside it, or a written reason it does not.

Known gaps, as a starting list rather than the answer:

- needs: is edited by hand. There is no command to add or drop a
  dependency, and it is line 2 — the line the takeable scan depends on.
- Re-parenting is a mv. Nothing stops that mv landing an open ticket inside
  a closed tree; check reports it afterwards, which is the wrong end.
- The title is line 3, edited in the editor or with sed. No pidly title.
- A field cannot be set from the CLI at all (25).
- There is no pidly show. Reading one ticket is cat, or the awk idiom for a
  bounded read, which is exactly the kind of thing a command should own.
- list is open-only. There is no way to ask what closed, or what closed
  recently, without find or git.
- Nothing searches bodies. bd has search; pidly has grep -r, which is fine
  until an agent has to know to exclude c/.
- Bulk anything: closing several tickets, or setting a field across a
  selection, is a shell loop.
- Moving a ticket between two trackers is a mv plus a registry entry by
  hand, and the numbers will collide.

Three outcomes per item, and the third is a real answer: a command exists, a
command should exist and here is its ticket, or this stays manual on
purpose. Deleting a ticket is the model for the third — it is deliberately
not a command and never will be.

Where a command is added, the reference must keep documenting the hand form
beside it. The commands are the convenient path; find, sed and mv are the
guaranteed one, and a command that quietly becomes the only way to do
something breaks the first property while satisfying the second.

Done when every operation in the reference and every gap above has one of
the three answers, the gaps worth closing are child tickets of this one, and
the reference and the command set agree with each other.

The test to apply: any bash a human or an agent writes to interact with
tickets is a missing command. Not "could pidly do this" but "did someone
have to write a loop".

This session is the evidence, and these are the commands actually typed
while working in this tracker, each of them a gap:

  find .pidly -type d -name 17            resolve a number to a path, over
                                          and over, because nothing prints it
  cat .pidly/2/11/info                    read one ticket
  sed -i '3a effort: M\narea: cli'        add fields to a ticket new just made
  sed -i '3s/.*/title: .../'              retitle 28
  sed -n 's/^created: //p' <path>/info    read one field from one ticket
  for n in 1 7 17 23; do ... done         read one field from several
  find .pidly -name info | while read f;  list number, claim and title
    do sed -n 1p; sed -n 3p; done         together, which no view prints
  awk 'NR>3 {if($0=="")exit;print}'       read the field block
  mkdir -p .pidly/c && mv .pidly/2 ...    close, before close existed

The last one is the pattern to notice: it stopped appearing the day 4
landed. Every other line above is still the only way to do that thing.

Your example — "what is the average effort across my open tickets" — is 27
plus arithmetic: one command hands over every effort value with its number,
and the agent does the rest. That is the line this audit should draw and
then hold. pidly hands over data in one pass; it does not compute
statistics, and it does not grow a query language. The reason is not purity:
a tally is one shell pipeline over well-formed output, and every aggregate
pidly owned would be one someone else wanted differently.

The other line worth holding is that the wrapper stays thin. Each of these
is a command over the format, not a new abstraction on top of it — pidly
path, pidly show, pidly title, pidly needs, pidly move — and every one of
them must still print, or do, exactly what the hand idiom in the reference
does.
